 ## Background
-The optimization of transfer functions has evolved from manual interaction to automated, data-driven approaches [S1]. Early methods focused on histogram analysis and clustering [S1]. With the advent of differentiable rendering and deep learning, methods like [Lookup TF Optimization](6791-lookup-tf-optimization.md) emerged, allowing for end-to-end learning of TF parameters from reference images [S1]. Sensitivity analysis, such as [Scalar Value Sensitivity Fields](7666-scalar-value-sensitivity-fields.md) and [Region Sensitivity Plots](7666-scalar-value-sensitivity-fields.md), provides a bridge between automatic optimization and user-guided editing by quantifying the impact of TF changes [S2]. Multi-dimensional TF design has been addressed through dimensional reduction techniques like SOMs [S1]. Time-varying contexts have extended these methods to handle dynamic data [S1], while Mixed Reality contexts incorporate biometric inputs for ROI selection [S1].
-
+The optimization of transfer functions has evolved from manual interaction to automated, data-driven approaches [G]. Early methods focused on histogram analysis and clustering [G]. With the advent of differentiable rendering and deep learning, methods like [Lookup TF Optimization](6791-lookup-tf-optimization.md) emerged, allowing for end-to-end learning of TF parameters from reference images [G]. Sensitivity analysis, such as [Scalar Value Sensitivity Fields](7666-scalar-value-sensitivity-fields.md) and Region Sensitivity Plots, provides a bridge between automatic optimization and user-guided editing by quantifying the impact of TF changes [S2]. Multi-dimensional TF design has been addressed through dimensional reduction techniques like SOMs [S1]. Time-varying contexts have extended these methods to handle dynamic data [7129-time-varying-transfer-function-optimization.md], while Mixed Reality contexts incorporate biometric inputs for ROI selection [8627-mr-tf-approach.md].
